A||N||5||Mickey_Moose||electrical short?!?||||9||
Z||000000||Mickey_Moose||05-05-2001||04:54 PM||||can anyone help?<P>started off when the power trunk was activated, fuse was blown (worked several times before), now as soon as you install a new fuse it blows right away. Have disconnected the trunk lid harness and have disabled power going to the trunk light/etc (cut the wire just before it goes through the firewall). Have also noticed another problem (related), the lower courtesy lights are always on (over head light is not due to the fuse blowing all the time) - they do partially dim when the doors are closed. I have gone through all the door switches and wiring, checked the wiring to the power door locks and all the way up to the top dome lights (no power mirrors) and am now at a lost. Any suggestions 1988 GT V6<P>thanks, Tim||||reg||9
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-05-2001||09:09 PM||||The courtesy lights staying on sounds like another "Floating-Ground" gremlin to me.  Check all your other fuses, sometimes a blown one from a different circuit could be the cause.<P>Look for shorts to your trunk release also at the back of the switch, and at the relay.  You'll find the trunk relay under the dash, right near the steering column.  It looks just like the horn relay, probably will be white (not sure though on color)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mickey_Moose||05-05-2001||10:03 PM||||thanks for the suggestions - what I have found so far by disconnecting each power lead one at a time from the distrubution block (connector block drivers side fender up above the fuse panel), was the one 2 prong connector that is closest to the front of the car having the problem (the next one back is for the over head dome/map lights) - with it disconnected, everything works except the dome light (I'm going to assume for now that it supplies power for the dome?). I trace it back quickly and it joins up into a large harness that runs under the guages. Will have to spend more time anotehr day to figure out what this wire is exactly. Unless someone here know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>thanks, again<BR>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||05-05-2001||11:10 PM||||The orange and white wires are indeed for the overhead lights.  This scan out of my 87 Helm's manual may also help you determine which wires you are looking at:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/whiteboxpinout_1.JPG"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/wirechart_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||05-06-2001||08:25 AM||||Most decent tool dealers (I think NAPA has them too) sell a short finder. Basically you get a +/-10A circuit breaker and a small inductive ammeter. You plug the breaker into the fuse socket that keeps blowing. It starts clicking like a turn signal as it overloads, opens, then resets. You follow the wiring with the ammeter which will "twitch" every time the breaker cycles. When you pass the location of the short, the needle stops moving with the breaker. The tool will work thru trim and moulding (no need to remove sill plates or cut up carpeting). Of course you waste time with that after looking for obvious stuff like broken off door switches and coins in the lighter socket  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||05-06-2001||09:51 AM||||thanks for those scans, they are of great help - looks like I probably should get a Helms manual as well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Looks like it may have to wait till next weekend for me to check out further, but at least I can drive the car for now. Interesting though that when the supply wire is plugged into the block that is when the fuse goes (even with the overhead light wire unplugged) - maybe there is a problem within the block itself?<P>short tool - sounds like a good thing to add to the old tool box, will have to check it out - with the way the car is now, shouldn't take too long for the breaker to reset since it blows a 25 amp fuse as fast as you can put it in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>thanks for all the help, saves me some hair and it was an afternoon 'well wasted' <P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
